⚙️ Auto Scaling Group (ASG) – Complete Deep Notes
🧩 1. What is Auto Scaling Group (ASG)?

An Auto Scaling Group (ASG) automatically manages the number of EC2 instances in a group based on demand.

✅ Main Purpose:
To maintain the right number of EC2 instances to handle the load.

💡 Example:

If CPU usage increases beyond 70%, ASG launches new EC2 instances.
If usage drops, it terminates unnecessary instances → saving cost.

🧠 2. Key Components of Auto Scaling
Component	Description
Launch Template / Launch Configuration	Defines how new EC2 instances will be created (AMI, instance type, key pair, SG, etc.)
Auto Scaling Group (ASG)	Logical group of EC2 instances managed together
Scaling Policies	Rules that define when and how to scale
Health Checks	Automatically detect unhealthy instances
Elastic Load Balancer (ELB)	Distributes traffic among instances in ASG
🧰 3. Launch Template (Recommended)

A Launch Template contains configuration to create EC2 instances automatically.

🧩 Includes:

AMI ID

Instance Type

Key Pair

Security Group

User Data (startup script)

IAM Role

🪜 Steps: Create Launch Template

Go to EC2 → Launch Templates → Create template

Enter:

Name: my-launch-template

AMI: Amazon Linux 2

Instance Type: t2.micro

Key pair: select existing

Security Group: allow HTTP (80)

Add User Data:

#!/bin/bash
sudo yum update -y
sudo yum install httpd -y
echo "<h1>Hello from Auto Scaling Instance $(hostname -f)</h1>" > /var/www/html/index.html
sudo systemctl start httpd
sudo systemctl enable httpd


Create template ✅

🏗 4. Create Auto Scaling Group (ASG)
🪜 Steps:

Go to EC2 → Auto Scaling Groups → Create

Choose Launch Template → select my-launch-template

Set Group Name: my-auto-scaling-group

Choose VPC & Subnets (min 2 AZs) for High Availability

Attach to Existing Load Balancer (Optional) → select ALB or NLB

Configure Group Size:

Desired Capacity: 2

Minimum Capacity: 1

Maximum Capacity: 4

Add Health Check Type: EC2 + ELB

Add Scaling Policies (explained below)

Review → Create ✅

🔁 5. Scaling Policies

Auto Scaling policies define when to add or remove EC2 instances.

📈 1. Target Tracking Policy

Maintains a specific metric target.

Example:

Target metric: Average CPU Utilization

Target value: 60%

👉 If CPU > 60% → add instance
👉 If CPU < 60% → remove instance

✅ Easiest and most commonly used.

⚙️ 2. Step Scaling Policy

Scale in steps based on thresholds.

Example:

Metric	Action
CPU > 70%	Add 1 instance
CPU > 85%	Add 2 instances
CPU < 40%	Remove 1 instance
🧮 3. Simple Scaling Policy

Trigger scaling action based on CloudWatch Alarm.

Example:

If CPUUtilization > 80% for 5 minutes → Add 1 instance

⚡ 4. Scheduled Scaling

Automatically scale at specific times (e.g., business hours).

Example:

Increase to 4 instances at 9 AM

Decrease to 1 instance at 9 PM

🧠 6. ASG Health Checks

ASG automatically replaces unhealthy EC2 instances.

🔍 Types:

EC2 Health Check: if instance is stopped or terminated → replaced.

ELB Health Check: checks if instance fails load balancer’s health check.

🌍 7. ASG + Load Balancer Integration

When ASG integrates with ELB:

New EC2 instances are automatically registered with Load Balancer.

If instance fails → removed from ELB + replaced by ASG.

✅ Ensures seamless traffic routing & fault tolerance.

💾 8. Instance Refresh

Allows you to update running instances with a new Launch Template version:

Rolling update

No downtime

Old instances terminated gradually

Example:
When you update AMI in Launch Template → ASG replaces instances one-by-one.

🧱 9. Lifecycle Hooks

Lifecycle hooks let you run custom actions when instances:

Launch (Pending:Wait)

Terminate (Terminating:Wait)

💡 Example: run configuration script or send notification to SNS before terminating.

⚙️ 10. Cooldown Period

Cooldown period = waiting time before triggering next scaling action (default 300 seconds).

✅ Prevents too many scale-in/out actions in a short time.

💸 11. Cost Optimization

Use Spot Instances with ASG to reduce cost.

Configure Mixed Instance Policy → combine On-Demand + Spot instances.

Example:
50% On-Demand, 50% Spot → same performance, 70% cheaper.

🧮 12. ASG Metrics (via CloudWatch)
Metric	Description
GroupInServiceInstances	Running healthy instances
GroupPendingInstances	Instances being launched
GroupTerminatingInstances	Instances being terminated
GroupDesiredCapacity	Desired number of instances
GroupTotalInstances	Total instances managed
🧹 13. Delete Auto Scaling Group

Go to EC2 → Auto Scaling Groups

Select your group → Actions → Delete

Confirm deletion
💡 Tip: Delete Launch Template separately if not needed.

🧩 14. Real-Life Use Case Example

Scenario: E-commerce website with variable traffic.

During sale events → traffic spikes

At night → low traffic

✅ Solution:

ASG minimum = 1, maximum = 6

Target tracking = 50% CPU

Connect to ALB

Result → automatic scaling without manual intervention.

🧠 15. Interview Questions – Auto Scaling Group (ASG)
❓ Question	💡 Answer
What is Auto Scaling?	Automatically adjusts EC2 capacity based on demand.
What is Launch Template?	Blueprint for creating EC2 instances in ASG.
Difference between Launch Config & Launch Template?	Template supports versioning, mixed instances; Config is legacy.
What are Scaling Policies?	Rules that control how and when to scale.
What is Target Tracking Policy?	Maintains metric target like CPU = 60%.
What is Step Scaling?	Adds/removes instances in steps.
What is Scheduled Scaling?	Scaling based on specific time or date.
What are Lifecycle Hooks?	Custom actions before/after instance launch or terminate.
What is Health Check in ASG?	Detects and replaces unhealthy instances.
Difference between EC2 & ELB health check?	EC2 = instance state; ELB = traffic health.
What is Cooldown Period?	Time delay to prevent rapid scaling.
What is Instance Refresh?	Rolling update of instances in ASG.
What is Desired, Min, Max Capacity?	Desired = target count, Min = minimum, Max = upper limit.
What happens when instance fails?	ASG terminates it and launches a new one.
Can ASG use multiple instance types?	Yes, via Mixed Instance Policy.
Does ASG work across AZs?	Yes, distributes across multiple AZs.
How does ASG help in cost optimization?	Scales down during low usage, can use Spot Instances.
What is the default cooldown period?	300 seconds (5 minutes).
How do you update Launch Template version in ASG?	Edit ASG → choose latest version → Instance Refresh.
Can ASG work without Load Balancer?	Yes, but less fault-tolerant.
